---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import ServiceEvidencePanel from '../components/ServiceEvidencePanel.astro';
import serviceEvidence from '../data/serviceEvidence.json';
import LegacyHeader from '../components/LegacyHeader.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ButtonPrimary from '../components/ButtonPrimary.astro';
import ButtonSecondary from '../components/ButtonSecondary.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How much does SEO cost in Australia?',
    answer: "There is no rate card: every Australian fee is set by the work your site actually needs. After reviewing your site, your market and who will implement the changes, we send a written proposal in AUD that lists the deliverables, the responsibilities on each side, the review points and the terms. Content volume, development work and local SEO requirements move the scope most. When you compare quotes, compare the included work line by line. A headline monthly price says very little about what will be done."
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does SEO take to work in Australia?',
    answer: "Expect early movement in impressions and long-tail rankings within 3 to 6 months, and meaningful traffic and lead growth over 6 to 12 months in competitive niches. Our own Australian evidence fits that range: Speediance Australia recorded 66% more organic clicks in the last six months than in the previous six. Your pace depends on your starting point, your competition and the state of the site, which is why the first fortnight goes on an audit. Treat any offer of page-one rankings in 30 days as a warning."
  },
  {
    question: 'What is included in your monthly SEO services?',
    answer: "Whatever the proposal lists, and nothing vague. It names the technical fixes, page edits, content, outreach and reporting in scope, says who implements each change (our team or your developer) and sets the review dates. The mix changes with the job: a migration needs redirect mapping and launch checks, while an ongoing content programme needs briefs, drafts and edits. Each month you receive a written report covering the completed work, the changes in your search data and the priorities for the month ahead."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you lock clients into long contracts?',
    answer: "Contract length is set for each client rather than taken from a standard form. The engagement length and notice period are written into your proposal beside the scope and the fee, so you can read them before you commit. Check four things before signing with us or anyone else: the proposed term, the notice requirement, who holds the account access, and what is handed over if the work ends."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you guarantee number-one rankings on Google?',
    answer: "No, and you should walk away from anyone who says yes. Google's results shift constantly and nobody outside Google controls them. Our case studies make the same point in writing: they publish the real numbers, state what the data cannot show and decline to promise the next client an identical result. What we commit to is the agreed work, reporting drawn from your own Search Console and analytics, and a straight account of what is and is not working."
  },
  {
    question: 'Is SEO still worth it with AI Overviews and ChatGPT?',
    answer: "Yes, because the same foundations serve both. Conventional search and AI answers each depend on crawlable pages, clear business information and content a buyer can use. Our published AI-search case studies report referral traffic and visibility observations between August 2025 and January 2026, and each one separates referral sessions from confirmed enquiries. Those observations are evidence of what we measured for those businesses. They are not a guarantee of rankings, AI citations or enquiries for yours."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you actually understand the Australian market and google.com.au?',
    answer: "Judge us on the published work. For Speediance Australia, organic clicks grew from 6,550 to 10,900 in a site-wide comparison of the last six months against the previous six for the Australian storefront, and we ran a separate campaign for the New Zealand storefront at the same time. That campaign meant researching Australian fitness-equipment searches, working against established equipment retailers on google.com.au and writing for both commercial and retail buyers. We deliver from the Philippines, so where state rules or trade licensing matter, your team checks the copy before it goes live."
  },
  {
    question: 'How does the time difference with the Philippines work?',
    answer: "Manila is UTC+8 and has no daylight saving. That puts us two hours behind Brisbane all year, two hours behind Sydney and Melbourne in winter (AEST), three hours behind them in summer (AEDT), and on the same clock as Perth. Our office hours are Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 18:00 Manila time, which is 11:00 to 20:00 in Sydney on AEST and 12:00 to 21:00 on AEDT. We agree a meeting window around your city and the season. Tasks follow agreed delivery dates, with time allowed for access, implementation and review, so a morning brief is not a promise of delivery that afternoon."
  },
  {
    question: 'Who does the work, and do you outsource anything?',
    answer: "The people on our about page do the work. We are a British-led team based in Quezon City. Sassin Lowe is our owner and founder, and Ben Lowe is our SEO Director, leading strategy and senior review. Jani A. Cañonero is Head of Team and Jewe O. Manalo is our SEO Expert. We also provide white label SEO fulfilment for other agencies, so we know how often a second layer sits between a budget and the work. When you hire us directly there is no such layer: the team named in your proposal is the team that delivers."
  },
  {
    question: 'How do you measure and report results?',
    answer: "We report monthly from GA4 and Google Search Console, the sources behind our published case studies, with rank tracking in SE Ranking. Where enquiry or sales tracking is reliable, we tie the search numbers to those actions. Rankings on their own do not pay anyone. Every report states what we did, what moved and the plan for next month, in plain English. Report calls are booked inside the overlap between your working day and ours, never at midnight your time."
  },
  {
    question: 'Will my content and links actually be Australian?',
    answer: "Yes. Content for Australian campaigns is written in Australian English (optimisation, not optimization), uses the terms your buyers use and is edited by a person before delivery. For links we look first at AU-relevant sources: Australian publications, industry directories, and supplier and partner sites. The Speediance Australia campaign pursued links from local fitness communities, blogs and directories. The case study describes that outreach without listing individual placements, so ask us for prospect examples from your own sector during scoping."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you also cover New Zealand?',
    answer: "Yes. Speediance New Zealand is a published case: 927 to 2,570 site-wide clicks, comparing the last six months with the previous six. The source screenshot does not show exact dates or a country filter. For a business serving both markets, we review each storefront, its offer and its search data separately before agreeing page changes, because shared products do not make the two campaigns interchangeable."
  }
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="SEO Services for Australian Businesses | Bright Forge"
  description="SEO for Australian businesses from our Philippines team, two to three hours behind Sydney. Published Australian results, AUD proposals, named specialists."
  canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/seo-services-australia/"
  type="service"
  faqs={faqs}
  geoRegion="AU"
  geoPlacename="Australia"
  contentLanguage="en-AU"
>
  <LegacyHeader slot="header" />

  <Hero
    badgeIcon="fas fa-globe-asia"
    badgeText="SEO for Australian Businesses"
    title="SEO Services for Australian Businesses With <span>Clear Scope and Local Context</span>"
    breadcrumbCurrent="SEO Services Australia"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <p>Speediance Australia took organic clicks from 6,550 to 10,900 in the last six months against the previous six, measured in Search Console, while we ran its SEO. We publish results like that with their source and their limits, so you can check them before you talk to us. We work with Australian ecommerce brands, SaaS companies and service businesses on google.com.au: technical SEO, Australian-English content, AU-relevant link building and reports written in plain English.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="story">
      <p>We deliver from Quezon City in the Philippines, two to three hours behind Sydney and Melbourne and on the same clock as Perth. Our office opens at 11:00 Sydney time in winter and 12:00 in summer, so calls and handovers are agreed inside your working day.</p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <ButtonPrimary href="/contact/?service=seo-services&market=AU#contactForm" text="Get an Australian SEO Proposal" />
      <ButtonSecondary href="#au-results" text="See the AU Numbers" />
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="visual">
      <div class="market-visual" aria-label="Australian SEO campaign snapshot">
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Your market</span>
          <strong>google.com.au, AU buyers, AU competitors</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node forge">
          <span>Your SEO team</span>
          <strong>Named specialists, 2 to 3 hours behind Sydney</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Your advantage</span>
          <strong>Agreed review windows and delivery dates</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <section class="proof-strip" aria-label="Australian SEO proof points">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="proof-grid">
        <div><strong>+66% organic clicks</strong><span>Speediance Australia, last six months against the previous six; average position weakened, 24.9 to 28.1</span></div>
        <div><strong>+177% organic clicks</strong><span>Speediance New Zealand, six-month comparison</span></div>
        <div><strong>2 to 3 hrs behind Sydney</strong><span>The same clock as Perth</span></div>
        <div><strong>5.0 on Google</strong><span>Public Google reviews</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="why-us">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why Australian businesses choose us</p>
        <h2>An SEO agency for Australia <span>whose proposal you can check line by line</span></h2>
        <p>Our published proof comes from this region. Speediance Australia's organic clicks grew 66% in the last six months against the previous six, and the separate New Zealand campaign grew clicks 177% over its own six-month comparison. Both case studies set out exactly what the figures cover and what they leave out.</p>
        <p class="lead">Most SEO proposals are hard to assess because they describe effort and leave out the work. Ours names the pages that need attention, who implements each change and what lands on your desk for review. A named team in the Philippines handles the strategy, the execution and the reporting.</p>
        <p>Calls are booked inside the working-hour overlap for your city. Australian agencies use the same team under their own brand through our <a href="/white-label-seo-services-philippines/">white label SEO fulfilment</a>, with client communication and approvals agreed before any work starts.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid small">
        <article class="card"><h3>Ecommerce brands</h3><p>Category, product and content SEO for brands up against marketplaces and established retailers on google.com.au. Our published Australian results come from this kind of store.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>SaaS &amp; platforms</h3><p>Comparison, feature and use-case pages for competitive Australian software terms, plus the authority building those pages need to rank.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Service businesses</h3><p>Service and location pages that turn local searches into enquiries, written only for the areas you really serve.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Australian agencies</h3><p>White label delivery under your brand: strategy, execution, quality checks and reports ready to send to your client.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="au-results">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Published results</p>
        <h2>Australian SEO results, <span>with the Search Console numbers attached</span></h2>
        <p>One fitness equipment brand, two storefronts, two coordinated campaigns. Both were measured in Google Search Console and both are published with the source screenshots, so you can read the figures and their limits for yourself.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="results-grid">
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Fitness Ecommerce · Australia</p>
          <h3>Speediance Australia</h3>
          <ul class="stat-list">
            <li><strong>Organic clicks:</strong> 6,550 → 10,900 <em>(+66%)</em></li>
            <li><strong>Impressions:</strong> 279,000 → 339,000 <em>(+22%)</em></li>
            <li><strong>CTR:</strong> 2.4% → 3.2% <em>(+33%)</em></li>
          </ul>
          <p>The work covered Australian keyword research, content and meta tag changes, technical SEO and local link building for speediance.com.au. The figures compare six months with the previous six across the whole Australian storefront: a site-wide view with no country filter, and the source screenshot does not show exact calendar dates or search filters. Clicks rose even though aggregate position worsened, from 24.9 to 28.1, and clicks are not confirmed sales.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/speediance-au-fitness-seo/">Read the full case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Fitness Ecommerce · New Zealand</p>
          <h3>Speediance New Zealand</h3>
          <ul class="stat-list">
            <li><strong>Organic clicks:</strong> 927 → 2,570 <em>(+177%)</em></li>
            <li><strong>Impressions:</strong> 46,600 → 85,800 <em>(+84%)</em></li>
            <li><strong>Avg. position:</strong> 31.3 → 25.0</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The NZ storefront is a separate campaign with its own keyword mapping for commercial gyms and individual buyers. Its results rest on their own six-month comparison and should be read apart from the Australian figures. They are site-wide search numbers; orders are tracked on their own.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/speediance-nz-fitness-seo/">Read the full case study →</a>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="results-note">Will your numbers match these? Nobody can say before seeing your site, and our case studies make no such promise. Read them for the changes reported, the work behind them and the limits of each source, then bring your own Search Console data to the first conversation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="services">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">What we deliver</p>
        <h2>Search engine optimisation services <span>scoped for the Australian market</span></h2>
        <p>Growing qualified Google traffic takes five things: a technically sound site, useful content, relevant pages, authority and honest measurement. Here is how we deliver each one for an Australian site, and where to read more.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="service-grid">
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-search"></i><h3><a href="/seo-audit-services/">SEO audits</a></h3><p>A technical, content and authority diagnosis against the Australian competitors you actually face, with the fixes ranked by commercial impact.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-key"></i><h3><a href="/keyword-research-services/">Keyword research</a></h3><p>Australian search demand mapped to specific pages, including the regional phrasing and intent differences a generic keyword export misses.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-sitemap"></i><h3><a href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/">Technical SEO</a></h3><p>Crawlability, indexation, site speed, schema and structure, with written handovers when your own developer makes the change.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-file-alt"></i><h3><a href="/on-page-seo-services-philippines/">On-page SEO</a></h3><p>Titles, internal links and page structure revised against what currently ranks in Australian results for your terms.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-pen-nib"></i><h3><a href="/content-seo-services-philippines/">Content SEO</a></h3><p>Australian-English content, edited by a person, mapped to local demand and planned around southern-hemisphere seasons.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-map-marker-alt"></i><h3><a href="/local-seo-for-google-maps-and-gbp/">Local SEO</a></h3><p>Google Business Profile, Australian citations and service-area pages for trades and local firms that depend on enquiries.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-link"></i><h3><a href="/backlink-seo-services-philippines/">Link building</a></h3><p>Outreach to Australian publications, directories and industry sites, each one chosen for its relevance to your buyers rather than bought as part of a bulk package.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-robot"></i><h3><a href="/ai-search-optimization-services-philippines/">AI Search Optimization</a></h3><p>Clear business facts and answer-ready pages, with checks of the answers and cited pages Australian buyers may see in AI Overviews and ChatGPT.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-exchange-alt"></i><h3><a href="/website-migration-services/">Website migrations</a></h3><p>Replatforming or moving to a .com.au domain, with redirect mapping, launch checks and post-launch monitoring to protect the rankings you have earned.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="process">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h2>Your first six months, <span>stage by stage, with agreed delivery dates</span></h2>
        <p>Every campaign starts with access, priorities and an agreed delivery schedule. The stages below are the usual sequence. Individual deadlines depend on the scope and on how quickly your team can review and release changes.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="process-grid">
        <article class="process-card"><span class="step-num">01</span><h3>Days 1–14 · Audit &amp; research</h3><p>We collect GA4 and Search Console access, then audit the site's technical health, content and authority against named Australian competitors. Australian keyword research follows. You receive a quick-wins list you could act on even without us.</p></article>
        <article class="process-card"><span class="step-num">02</span><h3>Weeks 3–6 · Strategy &amp; fixes</h3><p>The strategy is signed off on a call in your business hours. Priority technical fixes go live, page targets are locked and the first content briefs are written in Australian English.</p></article>
        <article class="process-card"><span class="step-num">03</span><h3>Months 2–3 · Build</h3><p>Content production, on-page rebuilds and AU-relevant link outreach run in parallel. Impressions usually move before clicks, and we show you that in Search Console data.</p></article>
        <article class="process-card"><span class="step-num">04</span><h3>Months 4–6 · Compound</h3><p>We review the pages and queries that changed, check that enquiries are being tracked correctly and revise the next set of priorities. The six-month Speediance comparison is evidence from one campaign, not a promised milestone.</p></article>
        <article class="process-card wide">
          <span class="step-num">∞</span>
          <h3>Every month after that</h3>
          <p>Senior strategy time, technical monitoring, content and links continue to the agreed scope. You receive a plain-English report drawn from GA4, Search Console and SE Ranking, with progress tracked in our <a href="/seo-command-centre/">SEO Command Centre</a>, and the review call is booked inside your business hours. Each report sets out the completed work, the movement in your search data and next month's priorities.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="pricing">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">How pricing works</p>
        <h2>How much does SEO cost <span>in Australia?</span></h2>
        <p>It depends on the work, so we price after reviewing your site. Below are the three scope decisions that shape an AUD proposal. They are not market-average rates and they are not fixed packages.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="price-grid">
        <article class="price-card">
          <p class="price-tag">Local &amp; single-location</p>
          <h3>Local search scope</h3>
          <p>We agree the real location or service area, who holds Business Profile access, which local pages are needed and who answers reviews. One location is a different job from several branches, and the proposal says which you are buying.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="price-card">
          <p class="price-tag">Established SMB</p>
          <h3>Managed campaign scope</h3>
          <p>We agree the priority pages, the technical fixes, the content required and the reporting. For every change, the proposal confirms whether our team or your developer implements it.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="price-card">
          <p class="price-tag">National &amp; competitive</p>
          <h3>National campaign scope</h3>
          <p>Larger catalogues and competitive national terms usually need more research, more content and more development support. We separate the immediate work from later phases so you can approve the budget in stages.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="price-note">
        <h3>What the proposal contains</h3>
        <p>Before you commit, you receive an itemised AUD proposal with the priorities, the deliverables and the terms. It identifies who implements what, and it lists the work that sits outside the scope so nothing is assumed. If the brief changes, we agree the change in writing before any additional work proceeds.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="team">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Who you'll actually work with</p>
        <h2>The offshore question, <span>answered with names and roles</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Our delivery team is British-led and based in Quezon City. Before work starts we tell you who handles strategy, who implements and who reviews, and which responsibilities stay with your own team.</p>
        <p>Ben Lowe leads strategy and senior review, Jani A. Cañonero is Head of Team and Jewe O. Manalo is an SEO specialist. Our <a href="/about-us/">about page</a> names the people behind the work. The same team delivers our <a href="/seo-services-philippines/">Philippines SEO services</a> and supports campaigns across the UK, the United States, Australia and New Zealand.</p>
        <div class="cred-strip">
          <div><strong>15+ years</strong><span>SEO Director experience</span></div>
          <div><strong>5.0 on Google</strong><span>Public Google reviews</span></div>
          <div><strong>Published case studies</strong><span>Real data, with source limits stated</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="quote-stack">
        <article class="quote-card">
          <blockquote>"We've been working with Bright Forge SEO for about three years now and they've been great. Really solid all-round SEO."</blockquote>
          <p class="quote-attr">Louise Maclean · Google review</p>
        </article>
        <article class="quote-card">
          <blockquote>"We've been working with Ben and Bright Forge SEO for over 18 months, with the primary focus being our SEO needs. They deliver excellent results."</blockquote>
          <p class="quote-attr">UK Auction List · Google review</p>
        </article>
        <article class="quote-card">
          <blockquote>"Their team is professional, transparent and genuinely invested in our success."</blockquote>
          <p class="quote-attr">Simon Kingsnorth · Business Owner</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="choose">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Buyer's guide</p>
        <h2>How to choose an SEO company <span>in Australia</span></h2>
        <p>Your shortlist may hold a Sydney agency, a freelance SEO consultant and an offshore team like ours. Put the same six questions to each. Under every question we give our own answer, so you can see what a complete reply looks like and hold us to it.</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="check-steps">
        <li><strong>What comparison sits behind the percentage?</strong> Ask for the two periods, the filters, the work completed and the limits of the source. Our Speediance Australia figure is a six-month, site-wide comparison with no country filter, and the case study records that average position got worse (24.9 to 28.1) while clicks rose. An agency that publishes its awkward numbers is easier to trust with yours.</li>
        <li><strong>Who will actually do the work?</strong> Some agencies pass delivery to a white-label team. We know because we are that team for some of them. Ask for the names. Ours are on the about page: Ben Lowe on strategy and senior review, Jani A. Cañonero as Head of Team, Jewe O. Manalo as SEO specialist.</li>
        <li><strong>What happens when a forecast is missed?</strong> Nobody controls Google, so a ranking guarantee is a sales line. A useful answer describes how misses are reported and how the plan changes. We commit to the agreed work and to telling you which parts of it did not help.</li>
        <li><strong>What does the report count?</strong> Search clicks are not sales, and our case studies say so. Ask whether enquiries and orders are tracked reliably before anyone reports on them. In months four to six we check that tracking ourselves before drawing conclusions from it.</li>
        <li><strong>What is in writing before money moves?</strong> You should see the term, the notice period, who holds each account and what is handed over at the end. Our proposals are itemised in AUD and list the work outside the scope as well as the work inside it.</li>
        <li><strong>If the team is offshore, what are its hours in your city?</strong> Ask for office hours converted to your local time, in summer and in winter. Ours are 9:00 to 18:00 in Manila: 11:00 to 20:00 in Sydney and Melbourne on AEST, 12:00 to 21:00 on AEDT, 11:00 to 20:00 in Brisbane all year and 9:00 to 18:00 in Perth.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="compare-grid">
        <article class="compare-card">
          <h3>Local agency or SEO consultant</h3>
          <p>The right choice when you need in-person workshops or someone on site. Ask who handles strategy, implementation and reporting, then compare the written scope with ours. We will not pretend to be in the room.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="compare-card">
          <h3>Remote delivery</h3>
          <p>Location does not settle the quality or the speed of the work. Check the named delivery team, a sample of real work, the approval process and the meeting windows before you decide.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="compare-card featured">
          <h3>The Bright Forge model</h3>
          <p>A named, British-led team working openly from the Philippines, with published AU and NZ campaign evidence, Australian-English content and itemised AUD proposals. Meeting windows and delivery dates are agreed for your campaign before work starts.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pf-section" id="onshore-or-philippines">
    <div class="pf-wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two ways to buy</p>
      <h2>An Australian agency or <span>a Philippines-based SEO team?</span></h2>
      <p class="pf-lede">Either can deliver strong SEO. What separates them is how the work runs day to day, and the table sets that out question by question.</p>
      <div class="pf-table-wrap"><table class="pf-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Onshore agency in Australia</th><th scope="col" class="pf-us">Bright Forge (Philippines-based team)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Working hours</th><td>Your business day, on your own state's clock.</td><td class="pf-us">Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 18:00 Manila time: 11:00 to 20:00 in Sydney on AEST, 12:00 to 21:00 on AEDT.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Meetings</th><td>Video or in person, including workshops at your office.</td><td class="pf-us">Video calls in an agreed window. We have no office in Australia.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Market knowledge</th><td>Staff live in Australia and know the market first hand.</td><td class="pf-us">We research the Australian market for each campaign. Where state rules or trade licensing matter, your team checks the copy.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Who does the work</th><td>Varies. Some deliver in-house and some pass work to a white-label team, so ask.</td><td class="pf-us">The named team on our about page, based in Quezon City and British-led.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Evidence to ask for</th><td>Dated comparisons from their own clients, with the source and its limits.</td><td class="pf-us">Published Speediance Australia and New Zealand results, plus case studies from other markets, each labelled with its market and its limits.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Proposal and billing</th><td>Normally quoted in AUD.</td><td class="pf-us">A written, itemised proposal in AUD. We publish no rate card.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Best suited to</th><td>Buyers who need people in the room or on site.</td><td class="pf-us">Buyers comfortable with scheduled calls and written handovers.</td></tr>
